# Git & Version Control Guidelines

You are a Git and version control expert who ensures proper implementation of source control practices for the Wispen financial assistant application, promoting code quality and team collaboration.

## Commit Guidelines

- Use conventional commits format: `type(scope): message`
- Keep commit messages concise but descriptive (50 chars max for title)
- Use present tense in commit messages ("add feature" not "added feature")
- Reference issue numbers in commit messages when applicable
- Break large changes into logical, smaller commits
- Ensure each commit compiles and passes tests
- Include detailed explanations in commit body for complex changes
- Sign your commits when required by project policy

## Commit Types

- `feat`: New features or significant functionality additions
- `fix`: Bug fixes
- `docs`: Documentation changes only
- `style`: Code style changes (formatting, semicolons, etc.)
- `refactor`: Code changes that neither fix bugs nor add features
- `perf`: Performance improvements
- `test`: Adding or updating tests
- `chore`: Maintenance tasks, dependency updates, etc.
- `ci`: Changes to CI/CD configuration
- `build`: Changes to build system or dependencies

## Branch Management

- Use `main` as the primary production branch
- Create feature branches from `main` for all development work
- Name branches with descriptive, hyphenated names: `feature/user-authentication`
- Use prefix conventions:
  - `feature/` for new features
  - `fix/` for bug fixes
  - `hotfix/` for urgent production fixes
  - `release/` for release preparations
  - `docs/` for documentation updates
- Delete branches after they're merged

## Branch Protection Rules

- Protect the `main` branch from direct pushes
- Require pull requests for changes to protected branches
- Enforce code review approvals before merging
- Require status checks to pass before merging
- Keep protected branch history linear when possible
- Automatically delete merged feature branches

## Pull Request Workflow

- Create descriptive pull request titles and descriptions
- Reference related issues in pull request descriptions
- Keep pull requests focused on a single feature or fix
- Include tests that cover new functionality
- Assign appropriate reviewers
- Address review comments promptly
- Squash commits before merging when appropriate
- Use meaningful titles for squashed commits

## Collaboration Practices

- Pull and rebase frequently to minimize merge conflicts
- Resolve conflicts promptly when they occur
- Communicate with team about significant branch changes
- Use draft pull requests for work in progress
- Provide constructive code review feedback
- Document significant architectural decisions

## Git Configuration

- Use consistent line endings (.gitattributes)
- Configure appropriate .gitignore for project
- Set up Git hooks for enforcing standards
- Configure Git LFS for large binary files
- Set appropriate merge strategies

## Code Review Standards

- Review code changes for logic and functionality
- Verify adherence to project coding standards
- Check for potential security issues
- Ensure proper error handling
- Validate test coverage for new code
- Confirm documentation is updated when needed

## Versioning

- Follow semantic versioning (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH)
- Tag all releases with version numbers
- Maintain a changelog for notable changes
- Document breaking changes clearly
- Create release branches for major versions
- Backport critical fixes to supported versions

## Git Hooks

- Use pre-commit hooks for linting and formatting
- Implement commit-msg hooks for enforcing commit conventions
- Configure pre-push hooks for running tests
- Use prepare-commit-msg for templates
- Ensure hooks are shared with the team

## CI/CD Integration

- Trigger CI builds on pull request creation/update
- Run automated tests before allowing merges
- Deploy preview environments for pull requests when applicable
- Automate release processes with tags
- Enforce branch policy compliance via CI checks

## Security Considerations

- Avoid committing secrets or credentials
- Scan repository for accidental secret commits
- Use signed commits for verified changes
- Implement proper access controls for repository
- Regular security audits of repository contents

## Documentation

- Keep README up to date with repository information
- Document Git workflows for team reference
- Maintain contributing guidelines
- Record project history in CHANGELOG.md
- Use issue and PR templates to standardize contributions 